[_] File the CCO term request for Action Specification $FOR THE HUMAN. Draft is ontology/upstream/cco-new-term-action-specification.md, in CCO's own term-request format; no duplicate upstream (searched 2026-09-30). Recommended: file it as is from your GitHub account (public, in your name). If CCO adopts it, v5 swaps IAO_0000007 for the CCO IRI and the ontology references CCO alone for actions. DROPPED 2026-09-30 by the human: 'dont worry about the action specification submission'. Draft removed from ontology/upstream/ (recoverable from git, ontology 4cc916e).$ !3 +human ^2026-09-30T23:17 %2026-09-30T23:45 #01a0f61c-a851-7216-92f5-0ce16e9c5c2c
[x] Decide how a dropped action is recorded $FOR THE HUMAN. Blocks competency question 6 (did we fulfil it, why not). A cancelled action has no act to give a status to. Recommended: a decision record (an act of planning with who, when and why, about the action) of the commitment kind; the New/Active/In Progress/Resolved/Closed chain shown to the user is derived from commitment, act and verification records. Real test case: the three ground-the-ontology actions cancelled 2026-09-30 because the first V5 proposal was withdrawn as implementation-first. ANSWERED 2026-09-30 by the human: a dropped action is just the cancelled state ('we are no longer doing this thing'); deletion means the action is out of the dataset entirely. Recorded as ontology Decision 4.$ !2 +human ^2026-09-30T23:43 %2026-09-30T23:53 #01a0f633-f621-77be-b5ae-7972859b136e
[_] Decide what an action shows when only its plan's act is recorded $FOR THE HUMAN. Found answering competency question 6: the weekly review was recorded as completed on the plan's act, so every one of its actions shows 'not started' (no record of its own). Options: (a, recommended) the view says 'done with the plan' when the plan's act is completed and the action has no record of its own, never inferring the parts happened; (b) completing a plan's act counts as completing its actions; (c) require a record per action. Answer a, b or c. CANCELLED 2026-10-01: a false question. The human: plans and actions each have their own state; neither is derived from the other. The weekly review example simply records the plan's act and no action acts, so its actions are correctly not started.$ !2 +human ^2026-09-30T23:56 %2026-10-01T00:20 #01a0f63f-c06e-734c-a861-9bbc7ae9cb69
[x] Answer the three open threads in ontology/V5_DESIGN.md $FOR THE HUMAN. Each has a proposal; 'yes' to a number settles it. (2) Values: a disposition in the person (BCIO personal value), a values statement is information about it, nothing new. (3) Can an action also be a plan? Yes, when it gets an objective of its own. (4) Recurrence is not an ontology entity: a recurring action prescribes many acts; the calendar rule is iCal format, owned by the spec. Settled: objectives have their own state (Decision 5); every action is part of a plan, the root charter included (Decision 2). DONE 2026-10-01: the human's earlier answers settled every thread; recorded as ontology Decisions 2, 5 and 6.$ !1 +human ^2026-09-30T16:03 %2026-10-01T00:28 #01a0f48f-400c-706b-9dce-d6717b17588c
[x] Distill V5_DESIGN.md into reference docs and retire it =distill-v5-design $Per the charter's Done when: settled choices in ontology/docs/DECISIONS.md, the domain and the CCO mapping as an evergreen reference doc, no open threads left in the note. Rewrite the ontology README for V5 in the same pass; it still describes v4 (actions:Charter, inServiceOf), which V5 contradicts.$ !1 ^2026-10-01T20:13 %2026-10-01T20:17 #01a0fa9a-5200-7795-8e15-2caaf403713c
[x] Decide how the spec represents the grounded domain $FOR THE HUMAN. Recommended: the spec represents V5 directly. Core projects every workspace straight to the CCO/IAO graph in docs/domain.md of the ontology; the spec states the mapping from each DSL, charter and objective field to its V5 pattern and ships SHACL shapes for that output (Decision 42); the CLI's saved queries are rewritten against V5 with their row shapes unchanged, so nvim does not notice; the v4 namespace is retired. Rejected: a compact ClearHead graph plus a published mapping to V5 (mints a vocabulary, against Decision 1, and two graphs to keep in sync); dropping RDF from the query path (gives up meaning in data). Cost: the graph is wordy, about 12 triples for an action with a priority, a context and an energy, and saved queries grow to match; the competency queries in ontology v5/queries are the working templates. The JSON-LD context stops being a contract: it cannot create the extra nodes V5 needs, so JSON-LD is serialized from the graph instead. Derived, overrule if wrong: blocked means waiting on something outside the data, a Performance Specification whose condition is the outside event; scheduled and due times are conditions too; the workspace vocabulary ws (source file and line) is not domain meaning and stays for now, with a search for standard terms planned before it is kept. Two related choices are queued separately: what plus-tags mean, and the root charter objective. Yes closes this and opens the implementation charter.$ !1 +human ^2026-09-30T16:03 %2026-10-01T20:21 <urn\:uuid\:01a0f48f-400c-706b-9dce-d6717b17588c #01a0f48f-4037-76ab-900b-9f2418ad04ec
[x] Decide what a plus-tag means\: context only, or also topic $FOR THE HUMAN. The DSL's plus-tag is meant as a GTD context, and V5 makes a context a condition: where, with what, or by whom an action can be done (Performance Specification). In practice tags are also topics: +wasm says what the action is about, not when it can be done, and would be mis-stated as a condition. Recommended: a plus-tag means context only, and the spec says so; a topic is what a charter or plan is for, so +wasm work belongs in a plan, and +human stays a context, since it says who can do it. Alternative: a second syntax for topic tags, mapped to a Descriptive ICE about the action; more surface, and topics overlap with charters.$ !2 +human ^2026-10-01T20:16 %2026-10-01T20:21 #01a0fa9d-6f37-7009-ac4e-a1770d1cfdc6
[x] Confirm how the root charter gets its objective $FOR THE HUMAN. V5 is strict: a plan without an objective is not a plan, and every charter is a plan, the root included. You said: a plan without an objective is just people daydreaming. Recommended, for the spec: a charter's Done when section counts as its objective; init seeds the root charter a maintained objective once, as it seeds the root alias, editable afterward; a charter with no objective is a gap that doctor reports, never a refusal to load. Charter frontmatter already has an objectives field linking objective files; both routes count.$ !2 +human ^2026-10-01T20:16 %2026-10-01T20:21 #01a0fa9d-6f47-7228-a7b1-c64d9e57fba5
[_] Move the SHACL shapes and JSON-LD context into the spec; the ontology keeps only meaning $Per platform Decision 42: move ontology/v4/actions-shapes-v4.ttl and actions.context.json into specifications/schemas beside the JSON schemas, with the examples that test shape and their tests; repoint specifications/ontology.md (which today names ontology/v4/* canonical). Delete ontology/v4/actions.schema.json: a stale second copy (2026-08-18, its own \$id) of the spec's pinned actions.schema.json, and the two differ. Examples that test meaning stay in the ontology. REVISED 2026-10-01: do not move the v4 files as they are. Moving them would put v4 meaning, which V5 retires, into the spec. If the representation decision lands as recommended, this becomes: write SHACL shapes for the V5 output in specifications/schemas, drop the JSON-LD context as a contract, repoint specifications/ontology.md at ontology docs/domain.md, and delete the v4 copies with the rest of v4. It then belongs in the implementation charter. SUPERSEDED 2026-10-01 by emit-the-ontology: v5-shapes writes new shapes for the V5 output, and retire-v4 deletes the v4 copies.$ !2 ^2026-09-30T21:55 %2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0f48f-4037-76ab-900b-9f2418ad04ec #01a0f5d0-ebb2-73bd-a381-d8e4ae301f9c
